libhsakmt (fmm) deferred-free re-map (gfx803, ROCm 10.0): the va-reuse-defer
patch's re-map of parked objects makes libhsakmt's GPUVM aperture
allocator hand out virtual addresses that the kernel still has mapped,
turning every code-object load into `HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES`.

WHY
---
vLLM 10.0 (torch 2.14) SIGSEGVs on its very first kernel launch on gfx803.
Root-caused end to end on the 10.0 line: the torch add kernel is
registered and found (the earlier "HIP functions_ map race" hypothesis
was wrong); GetStatFunc -> program.load() -> setKernels ->
executable_load_agent_code_object fails because the code BO's GPUVM map is
rejected by the kernel with EINVAL (`amdgpu_vm_bo_map`: "va ... conflict
with ...", verified in dmesg), which surfaces as
HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES; GetFunc then returns
hipErrorNoBinaryForGpu and ihipLaunchKernel's fallback
reinterpret_cast's the host stub as a hipFunction_t, whose "map" is the
stub's own code -> SIGSEGV in amd::Kernel::getDeviceKernel.

The colliding kernel mapping belongs to a freed buffer parked on the
va-reuse-defer FIFO. The defer park branch RE-MAPS the object
(_fmm_map_to_gpu) so a lagging reader of a freed buffer keeps hitting a
valid page; that re-established mapping stays in the kernel's GPUVM
interval tree, and the fmm aperture allocator then hands the same VA range
out again -> the kernel rejects the overlap. HSA-side changes only shift
the failure (HSA swaps, AllocateUncached reverts, deferred-FIFO length)
because the leak is the re-map itself. Removing the re-map eliminates the
collision. The freed object still keeps its VA reserved in the aperture
(the defer's whole point), so the only behavioral loss is the stale-reader
tolerance -- which this 10.0 line no longer needs because the
va-reuse-defer-mapping patch's re-map was the thing creating the stale
kernel state in the first place.

Also unmap before free in fmm_do_release: the kernel's
amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm_free_memory_of_gpu refuses to free a BO that is
still GPU-mapped (returns EBUSY); with the re-map gone an aged-out object
was never re-mapped, so the unmap is a no-op -- but it is what makes
fmm_do_release correct in isolation (a parked object that arrives mapped
would otherwise leak its kernel mapping and re-trigger the same collision).

Verified on real hardware: with this + d2h-null-dsthost, the full vLLM
10.0 Qwen3.5-2B bench (qwen35_2b_bench_v3.py) completes with EXIT=0
(prefill 311.0 tok/s, decode 30.2 tok/s, 2101-token prompt / 128 decode;
7.14 record for the same bench: 331.7 / 24.4).

Applies AFTER va-reuse-defer.patch (it edits the park branch and the
release path that patch introduced).

WHAT
---
Remove the _fmm_map_to_gpu re-map from __fmm_release's park branch, and
unmap before the BO free in fmm_do_release so an aged-out parked object
can never leave a stale kernel mapping behind.

2026-09-07 FIX: the unmap-before-free above used the wrong ioctl args
struct
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
A ComfyUI user (RX 580 2048SP, 8 GB) attached a standalone C++/HIP repro
(no PyTorch) to the same VRAM-retention report this patch's sibling
fmm-keep-userptr-map.patch was pulled for: hipMalloc + hipFree in a
loop never returns memory to hipMemGetInfo/mem_info_vram_used, and a
5-cycle allocate/free workload OOMs by cycle 2. Reproduced as reported
on the project's gfx803 box.

The "unmap before free" line this patch originally added passed
`struct kfd_ioctl_free_memory_of_gpu_args args` (8 bytes: one __u64
handle) to AMDKFD_IOC_UNMAP_MEMORY_FROM_GPU, which is macro-sized for
`struct kfd_ioctl_unmap_memory_from_gpu_args` (24 bytes: handle,
device_ids_array_ptr, n_devices, n_success -- confirmed against
/usr/include/linux/kfd_ioctl.h). The kernel's ioctl handler
(kfd_ioctl_unmap_memory_from_gpu in kfd_chardev.c) reads n_devices from
past the end of that 8-byte struct; the value found there is either 0
(handler returns -EINVAL immediately: "Device IDs array empty") or
uninitialized garbage read as a device-array pointer (copy_from_user
fails with -EFAULT). Either way the unmap ioctl's return value was not
even checked, so it silently did nothing. The BO stays GPU-mapped, so
the FREE_MEMORY_OF_GPU ioctl right after hits
amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm_free_memory_of_gpu's `if (mapped_to_gpu_memory > 0)
return -EBUSY` (confirmed by reading that function), which
fmm_do_release only logs via pr_err, not fatal -- so the VA and
vm_object are released from libhsakmt's own bookkeeping regardless, and
the kernel-level BO is orphaned until process exit.

The fix uses the existing, correct `_fmm_unmap_from_gpu()` helper
(same file) instead of hand-rolling the ioctl: it builds the real
kfd_ioctl_unmap_memory_from_gpu_args from object->mapped_device_id_array
/ object->mapped_device_id_array_size (populated at the object's
original map, since the park branch above no longer re-maps it), and
checks the ioctl's return value per handle.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (2026-09-07): debug-instrumented build shows
every deferred BO now unmaps and frees successfully (ioctl ret 0) where
the unfixed build never called FREE_MEMORY_OF_GPU with a chance of
success (confirmed via strace: zero occurrences across the whole repro
run). This closes a genuine correctness bug in the defer FIFO's own
drain path.

NOT A FULL FIX for the attached report: rerunning the same repro
against this fix still ends close to the unfixed baseline (7056 MB
retained vs. 8080 MB, both out of 8192 MB, still OOMs by cycle 2) even
though the debug instrumentation confirms essentially all allocated
bytes (about 8064 of ~8000+ MB requested) drain through fmm_do_release
successfully over the run. The remaining retention is not explained by
anything in libhsakmt: it survives a KFD ioctl that reports success, so
it points at the amdgpu kernel driver's own BO teardown (a lingering
reference keeping the BO's refcount off zero, or reclaim genuinely
lagging behind an allocate/free workload that gives the GPU no idle
time) rather than at this file. That part is still open.

diff --git a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
@@ -209,6 +209,10 @@ static const manageable_aperture_ops_t mmap_aperture_ops = {
 };

 static void fmm_defer_release(HsaKFDContext *ctx, manageable_aperture_t *aperture, vm_object_t *object);
+static int _fmm_unmap_from_gpu(HsaKFDContext *ctx,
+		manageable_aperture_t *aperture, void *address,
+		uint32_t *device_ids_array, uint32_t device_ids_array_size,
+		vm_object_t *obj);
 static HSAKMT_STATUS _fmm_map_to_gpu(HsaKFDContext *ctx,
 			manageable_aperture_t *aperture,
 			void *address, uint64_t size, vm_object_t *obj,
@@ -1009,6 +1013,16 @@ static void fmm_do_release(HsaKFDContext *ctx, struct deferred_release *e)
 	struct kfd_ioctl_free_memory_of_gpu_args args = {0};
 	uint32_t i;

+	/* The defer window keeps the object mapped from allocation time (the
+	 * park branch below no longer re-maps it, so this is its original,
+	 * still-live mapping). free_memory_of_gpu refuses a mapped BO with
+	 * EBUSY, so unmap first -- through the existing _fmm_unmap_from_gpu
+	 * helper, which builds the real kfd_ioctl_unmap_memory_from_gpu_args
+	 * (handle plus object->mapped_device_id_array) that
+	 * AMDKFD_IOC_UNMAP_MEMORY_FROM_GPU is macro-sized for.
+	 */
+	_fmm_unmap_from_gpu(ctx, e->aperture, e->object->start, NULL, 0, e->object);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < e->object->handle_num; i++) {
 		args.handle = e->object->handles[i];
 		if (!args.handle)
@@ -2433,9 +2447,6 @@ static int __fmm_release(HsaKFDContext *ctx,
 		 * fault. fmm_do_release() tears the mapping down for real when
 		 * the object ages out of the window.
 		 */
-		if (object->mapping_count == 0)
-			_fmm_map_to_gpu(ctx, aperture, object->start,
-					object->size, object, NULL, 0);
 		fmm_defer_release(ctx, aperture, object);
 	}

